    "collected_at": "2026-11-17T18:55:09.163246+00:00",
    "origin_id": "https://www.freightwaves.com/news/red-sea-overcapacity-looms-as-ocean-container-spot-rates-still-50-above-year-ago",
    "canonical_url": "https://www.freightwaves.com/news/red-sea-overcapacity-looms-as-ocean-container-spot-rates-still-50-above-year-ago",
    "origin_publisher": "FreightWaves",
    "published_at": "2026-11-17T16:28:34+00:00",
    "published_at_raw": "2026-11-17T16:28:34+00:00",
    "publication_precision": "TIMESTAMP",
    "acquired_at": "2026-11-17T18:56:33.784395+00:00",
    "available_at": "2026-11-17T18:56:33.784395+00:00",
    "body": "Ocean container freight rates remained elevated on major east-west trade lanes, even as container carriers’ growing return to the Red Sea and an expanding global fleet raised the prospect of downward pressure on prices in 2027.\nAsia-Europe prices were broadly stable for the week ending Nov. 13, while trans-Pacific rates edged higher, according to analyst and SONAR data contributor Freightos (NASDAQ: CRGO ). Congestion, higher fuel costs and transportation disruptions continued to support prices, but carriers’ attempts to secure further increases faced resistance.\nContainer carriers are continuing to restore Asia-Europe services through the Suez Canal and Red Sea, reducing their reliance on diverted voyages around Africa. That’s been the case since late 2023, when Houthi rebels in Yemen attacked shipping in support of Gaza. At the same time, observers say recent violence involving merchant vessels in locations ranging from the Black Sea to the Strait of Hormuz and Somalia are likely helping to support higher rates, as well.\nMaersk and Hapag-Lloyd announced on November 16 that their Gemini AE1, AE2 and AE3 services would return to the trans-Suez route, with the first departures under the revised arrangements scheduled for December and January. The changes remain dependent on continued stability in the region.\nShorter voyages reduce the time vessels spend completing service rotations, increasing effective shipping capacity. That shift coincides with substantial fleet growth, as Alphaliner figures put the containership orderbook at 15.12 million twenty-foot equivalent units, equivalent to 43.9% of the trading fleet, with fleet capacity projected to expand 9.2% in 2027.\nTogether, fleet expansion and shorter routes increase the risk that available capacity will outpace cargo demand, weakening carriers’ pricing power next year.\nFor now, lingering congestion and higher fuel costs continue to support Asia-Europe container prices, which the supplied market update places 40%–50% above year-earlier levels.\nAsia-North Europe rates were broadly unchanged at about $3,400 per forty-foot equivalent unit, or FEU, although they were down 5% from the end of October. Asia-Mediterranean prices were also approximately steady at $4,300 per FEU.\nThe stability suggests that carriers’ early-November general rate increases failed to take hold, raising doubts about another round of mid-month hikes.\nCarriers are responding to the demand lull by increasing blanked — delayed or cancelled — scheduled voyages to restrict available capacity. Published reports cite eight Asia-Europe sailings were scheduled to be blanked the following week, up from five.\nFrench dockworkers and port staff were called to join a 24-hour stoppage on November 17, while Spanish terminals were recovering from a November 11 general strike whose impact varied by location. While centered in Europe, job actions and other disruptions have a nasty way of dominoeing through the supply chain.\nFreightos noted that carriers are preparing further price increases for late November and December, with pre-Lunar New Year shipping demand potentially providing stronger support.\nTrans-Pacific rates remained near peak levels, edging up last week to $8,460 per FEU to the U.S. West Coast and $9,850 per FEU to East Coast hubs.\nOther benchmarks showed some softening. Drewry’s World Container Index recorded weekly declines on Shanghai-Los Angeles and Shanghai-New York services, while forwarders reported discounts below published rates, highlighting differences between market measures and individual bookings.\nDelays from inland challenges\nBeyond ocean pricing, inland transportation continues to pose challenges. Containers moving by rail through Los Angeles and Long Beach face delays, with record Asian import volumes and storm-related disruption on BNSF’s Southern Transcon network worsening Southern California rail dwell times.\nA November 20 strike deadline at LA-Long Beach involving watchmen with International Longshore and Warehouse Union Local 26 poses an additional operational risk, though contract bargaining continues.\nUS-China talks complicate the trade outlook\nPresident Donald Trump is scheduled to meet Chinese President Xi Jinping this week, ahead of the January 10 expiration of the interim US-China trade agreement.\nAlthough further de-escalation remains possible, divisions within the administration and renewed technology restrictions have added uncertainty. The Wall Street Journal reported disagreements among Trump’s advisers over the direction of China policy ahead of the summit. It’s also uncertain how China will approach dealmaking with the specter of a Democratic congress set to take control in January.\nWashington has also moved to restrict Chinese access to advanced American artificial intelligence chips through overseas computing infrastructure, adding another point of friction before the talks.\nThe outcome could influence shipping demand and the timing of cargo movements as businesses assess the durability of the trade agreement.\nPanama Canal eases restrictions as water conditions improve\nImproved rainfall has allowed the Panama Canal Authority to ease restrictions introduced after lower-than-normal precipitation over the summer.\nIn a November 9 advisory, the authority increased total daily booking capacity to 34 transit slots and restored the maximum authorized draft for vessels using the Neopanamax locks to 50 feet. It cited improved water conditions, favorable hydrological projections and water-saving measures.\nEl Niño remains a risk, although recent forecasts have reduced the probability of an exceptionally severe event. Bloomberg reported that the US Climate Prediction Center lowered the likelihood of the event exceeding the strength of the 1982–1983 season to roughly 60%, from 83%.\nFreightos sees a lower risk of severe canal disruption through the first half of 2027 than had been feared over the summer, although renewed restrictions remain possible.\nWhy it matters: Shippers face a market in which high current prices coexist with growing expectations of excess capacity. But cancelled sailings, labor disruption and inland congestion may continue to undermine reliability.\nRead more articles by Stuart Chirls here .\nRead more:\nTruck access and wharf upgrades part of $10.3B infrastructure plan at Port of New York and New Jersey\nU.S. container imports hit record 2.67 million TEUs in October as port delays rise\nTanker strikes cruise riverboat in New Orleans\nCMA CGM triples profit as Q3 freight rates and cargo volumes rise\nEvergreen Marine nine-month profit up 18% to $2.16 billion",
